---
name: pr-body
description: Write a pull request title and description. Use when you open a PR, when a push changes what a PR does, or when asked to describe a PR.
---

# PR Body

{{/* why: Warp's first line: "give the reviewer what the diff cannot".
Sentry: "a cover note for reviewers, not a changelog". The owner does not read
diffs: "i dont read diffs like that" (Codex, 2026-11-16). */ -}}
The description gives the reader what the diff cannot: why the change exists,
what it affects, which decisions were made, and where to look. Write for
someone who has not seen the conversation and does not read diffs.

## Before you write

{{/* why: Warp: commit messages often claim things about code that are
wrong, so check before you repeat them. The user's rule "check things
yourself". */ -}}
1. Read the whole diff against the base branch, the request, and the commit
   messages. Check each claim in a commit message against the code before you
   repeat it.
{{/* why: Warp: "the template's own instructions outrank this skill". Sentry
ignores templates and Matt leaves it to the repo; Warp's rule is the safer
default because a template is what that repo's reviewers asked for. */ -}}
2. If the repository has a PR template, fill it in. Its instructions come
   before this skill.

## Title

{{/* why: Theo's file-pr: the title usually becomes the commit message, with
this BAD/GOOD pair. Sentry also bans vague titles ("update", "cleanup"). */ -}}
3. Follow the repository's recent titles. Say what changes for the user, not
   how the code does it: "cut websocket frame size by 70%", not "negotiate
   permessage-deflate".
{{/* why: skill-studio #678 and sentry-devex #128 (2026-11-19) each did four
things under a title that named one. Matt splits large work into a stack
("Stack 6/9") with one change per PR. */ -}}
4. A PR does one thing. If it does more, split it into a stack of PRs, or
   name each change in the title and in the opening sentences.

## Body

{{/* why: Matt opens a migration PR with "ONE-WAY door ... Matt merges it",
and Kent's template has a `Door: two-way | one-way` field under the intent.
Dex: "Merging this does not fix npm. A new CLI release has to go out after
it lands." In sentry-devex #128 the step after the merge (make gate/risk a
required check) was hidden in a stray paragraph (2026-11-19). */ -}}
5. Start the body with up to three bold lines, each only when it applies:
   `Door: one-way` when the change is hard to undo (stored data, a public
   contract, deleted data), who merges it if not the agent and why, and what
   must happen after the merge.
{{/* why: Theo, Dex ("exactly one sentence") and Warp all open with the
problem. Warp: the first paragraph tells a reader whether the PR is theirs.
Issue link: Warp ("never invent a plausible ticket ID"), Sentry ("only when
verified") and Dex (ticket link on the first line). Added 2026-11-19 after
the side-by-side comparison. */ -}}
6. Then one to three plain sentences: what was wrong or missing, and what is
   true now. A reader of only this paragraph knows whether the PR concerns
   them. For a change to a process, a check or a rule, say what changes for
   whom and how often. If an issue exists, link it once. Never guess an issue
   number.
{{/* why: Matt's migration PR gives the size, the 4,117 generated lines,
and a reading order before the summary. The owner does not read diffs, so
the order matters most when a PR is large. */ -}}
7. When the diff is over about 300 lines, give its size, how much of it is
   generated, and the order to read the files in.
{{/* why: Dex's describe-pr and Matt's pr both build the outline from
show-me ("the smallest view that makes the key point clear"). It lets the
owner see the shape of the change without reading the diff. The HTML option
in show-me is for chat, not for a Markdown PR body. */ -}}
8. Show the shape of the change with the smallest view from the `show-me`
   skill (`~/.agents/skills/show-me/SKILL.md`): pseudo-code, a call tree, a
   component tree, a file tree, a Mermaid diagram, or a before and after. Use
   one view, sometimes two, each next to the sentence it supports. Do not
   write an HTML file. When the change is text a person reads, such as a bot
   comment or a CLI message, paste the real output instead.
{{/* why: Warp: decisions with their rejected alternatives are "the
highest-value content in most descriptions and it cannot be recovered from
the diff". Dex lists "surprising decisions". */ -}}
9. Name each decision that had a real alternative, and why the alternative
   lost. Do not invent one.
{{/* why: Matt's Evidence section (screenshot first, then the test that
failed and now passes). Warp's honest validation: never write intent as a
result. 4 sessions of complaints that descriptions copied the check list. */ -}}
10. Prove it: a screenshot for a UI change, otherwise the test that failed
    before and passes now, or the command output that changed. If something
    did not run, say so once and name what should run. Never write an
    intention as a result. Do not list passing checks, because GitHub shows
    them.
{{/* why: Dex's "special things to note" (1 to 3, or none), Matt's "merge
danger" (one-way door, blast radius) and Warp's focus areas ("where you are
unsure" is "the single most valuable line"). The Nov 16 trim removed this
section without a reason. Renamed to Dex's heading on 2026-11-19, with
Matt's "Left out on purpose". */ -}}
11. Only when it applies, add one to three items under `Special things to
    note`: what can break beyond the changed files and for whom, what was
    left out on purpose, or the part you are least sure of. Name the file or
    the decision.
{{/* why: Theo ends every PR this way and Dex links the agent session. It
lets the owner compare which model writes code that needs fewer fixes.
Warp disagrees ("Co-Authored-By belongs in the commit"), but that is about
commit trailers, and Codex commits here carry none. */ -}}
12. End with `Created with <model> in <harness>.` Name every model that wrote
    part of the change.

## Cut

{{/* why: Warp's cut pass, word for word in intent: "name the decision it
helps the reviewer make. If you cannot name one, delete it." The list of
things to delete is Warp's anti-patterns plus Sentry's and Theo's bans. Size
anchors are Warp's. The user complained about long, dense text in 5
sessions. */ -}}
13. For each paragraph, name what it helps the reader decide. If you cannot,
    delete it. Delete file lists, a restated diff, the history of the branch,
    words such as "robust", "comprehensive" or "clean", and advice on how to
    review. A small change needs one paragraph. A focused fix or feature needs
    no more than about 400 words.

## Publish

{{/* why: Warp, Sentry and Matt all warn about stale descriptions. Warp: do
not append a revision log; describe the branch as it is now. Numbers: the
devex #128 trial run ("46 would stop") was measured before a later push let
migrations ship; the current gate gave 42 (2026-11-19). */ -}}
14. After a push that changes what the PR does, write the description again
    from the current diff. Run every number again, or delete it. Do not add a
    change log.
{{/* why: `gh pr create --body "..."` breaks Markdown that holds backticks or
quotes. Sentry and Dex both write a file first. */ -}}
15. When asked to open or update the PR, publish with
    `gh pr create --body-file` or `gh pr edit --body-file`. Otherwise give the
    text in your reply.
{{/* why: Sentry ("never include customer or organization names, user
emails... secrets, or PII") and Theo ("check the title, description, quotes,
and attachments for secrets and private details"). PR bodies are public in
public repos. Added 2026-11-19. */ -}}
16. Before you publish, check the title, body and screenshots for secrets,
    emails, customer names and other private details. Remove them.
